title: "HOP Pool: Move Metadata into a KV Store"

doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |
      Reworks `sc-hop`'s `HopDataPool` so entry metadata lives in a `parity-db` key-value
      store instead of per-entry `.meta` files plus a full in-memory index.

      - Metadata is SCALE-encoded into a btree-indexed `parity-db` column at
        `<data_dir>/meta-db/`, keyed by the entry's `blake2_256` content hash. Blob storage
        is unchanged: `<data_dir>/blobs/{00..ff}/<hash>.blob`.
      - Full metadata (recipients, ephemeral public keys, ack state) is no longer held in
        RAM. In-memory state is reduced to derived caches — pool byte total, entry count,
        per-sender usage, and a new expiry index — all rebuilt by iterating the column at
        startup.
      - The expiry index (`BTreeSet<(expires_at, hash)>`) lets `cleanup_expired` and
        `get_promotable` walk only the relevant time window instead of every entry in the
        pool on each maintenance cycle.
      - Read-modify-write paths (`insert`, `claim`, `ack`, `remove`, promotion bookkeeping)
        are serialized by a dedicated mutex, replacing the write lock over the old index.
      - Startup recovery drops rows with a non-32-byte key, an unsupported
        `HOP_META_VERSION`, or a SCALE decode failure, together with their blobs; orphan
        `.blob` and leftover `.tmp.*` files are reaped in the same pass.
      - A second column holds a database-wide schema version, reconciled on every open: a
        fresh database is stamped, one written by a newer binary is rejected rather than
        misread. Column-layout changes are applied while the database is closed, appending
        any columns an older layout is missing.
      - Leftover `<data_dir>/meta/` files are imported into the metadata column at startup
        and the tree is removed. `HopEntryMeta`'s encoding is unchanged, so this is a move
        rather than a conversion, and it runs before startup recovery so imported entries
        populate the caches and their blobs are not reaped as orphans.
      - New `HopError::Db(String)` variant (JSON-RPC error code `1022`) surfaces
        `parity-db` open, read, write, and decode failures, including an unsupported schema
        version.

      `HopDataPool`'s public method signatures are unchanged; the only breaking change for
      downstream code is the added `HopError` variant.

  - audience: Node Operator
    description: |
      HOP metadata moves from `<data_dir>/meta/{00..ff}/<hash>.meta` files into a
      `parity-db` instance at `<data_dir>/meta-db/`. The upgrade needs no operator action:
      existing `.meta` files are imported on the first start and the old `meta/` tree is
      removed, with imported and skipped counts logged. Entries whose `.meta` file is
      corrupt or has no matching blob are dropped, and their blobs reaped. Downgrading
      afterwards is not supported — an older binary finds no `.meta` files and reaps the
      whole pool.

      No CLI flags were added, removed, or changed, and `hop_poolStatus` reports the same
      fields as before. RPC clients may now see error code `1022` for metadata store
      failures, including a refusal to open a `meta-db/` written by a newer node version.

crates:
  - name: sc-hop
    bump: major
